-- Batches: a peptide inventory whose containers come from three places. A
-- PeptideBatch is a container of one peptide, and it was either fermented
-- from a bacteria strain, ordered from an outside vendor, or mixed from other
-- batches already on the shelf. The eleventh Velle spec, written to stress one
-- modeling question against a domain that needs it: polymorphism over a shared
-- record. In an object language this is a supertype with three subtypes, each
-- carrying its own fields; here it has to be said with shapes, a static union,
-- and refinements.
--
-- What the domain asks of the language, and where each answer lives:
--   · one record, three kinds, each kind with its own data — the hierarchy
--     inverted: the batch is one base shape holding what every batch has, each
--     kind of provenance is its own shape, and `origin: one Origin` points at a
--     static union of the three (Reference §6, "the polymorphic association";
--     §9). "Exactly one kind" costs nothing: a `one` field holds one value.
--   · the kind as a question, never a stored tag — refinements over the batch
--     narrowing by `origin is Fermentation` (Reference §10)
--   · the components of a mixture, each with how much was taken — an edge that
--     carries data is a shape of its own (Reference §6, the graduation point),
--     arriving inline with the composition as its closure (`with`)
--   · what a batch has left — a derivation over the components that drew from
--     it, read through the inferred inverse, never a mutated counter
--   · the books rules — a batch never claims the same origin as another, a
--     mixture is of one peptide, nothing draws more than a batch holds
--
-- What it cannot say, or says at a cost. Each is a probe in this example's
-- output module (`BatchesProbesTest.kt`) and a finding in OQ16's calibration
-- record (B1–B8); the one-commit arrival is OQ63.
--   · the direct translation. Three base shapes under a union share nothing
--     they declare separately (Reference §9), so a common field like the
--     quantity is unreadable from the union by the Reference. That is the
--     cross-shape mixin gap, OQ35. (The validator does not enforce the rule on
--     a predicate over the union today, and the runtime then resolves the name
--     per instance — the accepted-by-accident spelling Reference §9 rejects.)
--   · the batch and its origin in one commit. The batch points forward at its
--     origin, and a closure only creates parts that point back at their
--     container (Reference §6, "Closures are trees"), so the origin is committed
--     first and the batch second. Between the two the origin is on the books
--     unclaimed, which the spec can show (`UnclaimedOrigin`) but cannot forbid:
--     a `never` over it would refuse the origin's own commit.
--   · "a mixture never contains itself, however many steps removed". The
--     transitive sentence wants a derived set of ancestor batches, and every
--     spelling of that set reads a field through a `many` hop, which the
--     evaluator does not do. One step removed is a books rule (`never` below),
--     and the sequence of commits makes it the only reachable case.
--   · the inferred inverse of `PeptideBatch.origin` is spelled `peptideBatchs`
--     (Reference §6 pluralizes with a literal `s`), so the union declares the
--     view under the readable name (Reference §6, "Renamed and derived
--     collections").
--
-- Comment convention (as in payments.velle): plain comments tell the business
-- story; `velle:` comments note the language machinery on show.

-- ── Reference data ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

act Peptide {
    code: text
}

act BacteriaStrain {
    name: text
}

-- The fermenter process the lab runs; a batch fermented from a strain comes
-- out of one of these.
act FermenterRun {
    vessel: text
    startedOn: Date
}

act Vendor {
    name: text
}

-- ── The three kinds of provenance ────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Each kind of provenance is a durable act of its own, holding only what that
-- kind knows. None of them knows the peptide or the quantity: those belong to
-- every batch, so they live on the batch.

act Fermentation {
    strain: one BacteriaStrain
    run: one FermenterRun
}

act ExternalOrder {
    vendor: one Vendor
    orderItem: text
}

-- A composition arrives with its components: which batches were drawn from
-- and how much of each. The components are created in the composition's own
-- commit; the batches they draw from already exist and are named by reference.
-- velle: the act's closure (Reference §6, "Inline part creation") — `components`
-- is the inverse inferred from Component.composition
act Composition {
    mixedOn: Date
    drawnMg: decimal = sum(components, amountMg)
} with components

-- velle: the edge carries an amount, so it is a shape, not a bare `many`
-- (Reference §6, the graduation point). Its two `one` fields target different
-- shapes, so both inverses infer under their default names.
shape Component {
    composition: one Composition
    source: one PeptideBatch
    amountMg: decimal
}

never (Component where amountMg <= 0)

-- velle: a static union — a pure `or` of base shapes, so a legal field type
-- (Reference §6). It exposes `id` and the inverses inferred through fields
-- typed with it; everything a kind knows is reached by narrowing. The body
-- declares the inferred inverse under its readable name (the inferred one is
-- `peptideBatchs`), a derived view over the declared side (Reference §6,
-- "Renamed and derived collections").
shape Origin = Fermentation or ExternalOrder or Composition {
    peptideBatches: many PeptideBatch = (PeptideBatch where origin == this)
}

-- ── The batch ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- velle: `origin: one Origin` is the polymorphic association. A `one` field
-- holds exactly one value, so "a batch has exactly one kind of provenance" is
-- structural, with no exclusivity rule to write and none to forget.
act PeptideBatch {
    peptide: one Peptide
    quantityMg: decimal
    origin: one Origin
    -- what has been drawn from this batch into mixtures, and what is left
    -- velle: a derivation over the inverse inferred from Component.source
    consumedMg: decimal = sum(components, amountMg)
    remainingMg: decimal = quantityMg - consumedMg
    -- the kind, read off the origin, never stored
    -- velle: narrowing in a conditional; with three operands the final else
    -- is the third operand (Reference §10)
    kind: text = if origin is Fermentation then "fermented"
                 else if origin is ExternalOrder then "ordered"
                 else "composite"
}

never (PeptideBatch where quantityMg <= 0)

-- The three kinds, as refinements the rest of the spec and the read side use.
-- velle: `is <Operand>` on a union-typed field (Reference §10)
shape Fermented = PeptideBatch where origin is Fermentation
shape Ordered   = PeptideBatch where origin is ExternalOrder
shape Composite = PeptideBatch where origin is Composition

-- One origin, one batch: a fermentation or an order or a mixture yields one
-- container. Counting the arriving batch among its origin's batches refuses
-- the second claim at the door.
-- velle: the view declared on the union, read through the union-typed field
never (PeptideBatch where count(origin.peptideBatches) > 1)

-- A mixture is of one peptide: every component was drawn from a batch of the
-- peptide the mixture is labelled as.
-- velle: the filter runs over the declared shape and matches the composition
-- by comparison. The narrowed spelling — `origin is Composition and exists
-- (origin.components where ...)` — is refused today: narrowing is lost on a
-- filter's source path (OQ44 §3; probe B4). `this.` reaches the batch from
-- inside the filter.
never (PeptideBatch where exists (Component where composition == this.origin and source.peptide != this.peptide))

-- Nothing draws more than a batch holds. The components arrive in the
-- composition's commit, so this is checked once the whole closure has landed.
never (PeptideBatch where remainingMg < 0)

-- A mixture never draws from the batch it produces. One step removed is a
-- books rule over the component. The transitive sentence is not evaluable
-- (see the header and `BatchesProbesTest.kt`), and does not need to be: a
-- component's source is a batch that existed before the composition, and the
-- composition exists before the batch it becomes, so a batch can only ever
-- draw from batches older than itself.
never (Component where source.origin == composition)

-- ── What happens as batches are used up ──────────────────────────────────────

-- The origin side of the two-commit arrival, as data: an origin on the books
-- that no batch has claimed yet. Visible, and reportable; not forbiddable.
shape UnclaimedOrigin = Origin where peptideBatches is empty

-- A batch drawn down to nothing is depleted; the lab is told once per
-- depletion, with the batch's kind in the notice.
-- velle: entry by a series creation read through an inferred inverse — a
-- Composition's closure landing, not a write to the batch's own columns
shape Depleted = PeptideBatch where remainingMg <= 0

shape DepletionNotice {
    batch: one PeptideBatch
    kind: text
    noticedOn: DateTime
}

rule NoteDepletion when Depleted {
    DepletionNotice from { batch: this, kind: kind, noticedOn: now }
}
